00:00:59.174 --> 00:01:00.410
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Awesome

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.120
JESSE ENDAHL: So
this talk assumes

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.600
that you have some knowledge
of containers and Kubernetes

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.550
and focuses on
practically protecting

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.355
your workloads in GKE.

00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:10.730
Specifically,
we're going to dive

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.710
into what's your
responsibility vs. what

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.840
Google handles for you.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.200
I will say also, just in
general, security in the cloud

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.810
is that shared responsibility
between your cloud

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.030
provider and you.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.910
Google does a lot of things
to protect the underlying

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:29.360
infrastructure, like
encryption at rest by default,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.100
and also providing
capabilities to you

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.340
that you can use to protect
your workloads, like I am.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.818
As newer infrastructure
models emerge,

00:01:36.818 --> 00:01:39.110
it's not always easy to figure
out what you're actually

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:39.920
on the hook for.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.380
So that's really what we're
diving into on this talk.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.363
So let's jump in and review
some Kubernetes basics,

00:01:45.363 --> 00:01:47.780
from a security point of view,
to make sure that we're all

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.605
on the same page.

00:01:50.605 --> 00:01:52.730
So within a Kubernetes
cluster, there's only really

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.577
three core, fundamental pieces.

00:01:54.577 --> 00:01:56.660
These are the parts of the
diagram that you see in

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.730
dark gray, and let's
walk through them

00:01:58.730 --> 00:01:59.742
from the bottom up.

00:01:59.742 --> 00:02:01.700
So at the bottom layer,
you've got the storage.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.200
That's etcd.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.600
Think of this like the
Kubernetes hard drive.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.340
Next up, you've
got the master that

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.078
controls your entire cluster.

00:02:09.078 --> 00:02:11.120
And then the next layer
up, you've got the nodes.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.510
That's the virtual machines
that are running your pods,

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.150
and pods are just collections
of one or more containers.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.650
So let's now walk
through the damage

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.890
that can be done at each
layer, from the top down.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.600
So the top layer, you've got the
containers, again, in the pod.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.700
If an attacker can
control a container,

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.590
they can do things
like abuse compute

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.200
to do things like mine
cryptocurrency, for example,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.350
schedule arbitrary
workloads on the node.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.170
If they're able to
escape the container,

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.090
potentially access
customer data.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:44.240
And if there isn't a strong
security boundary there,

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.330
they could potentially
escape to the node.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:51.030
From the node, if an
attacker can control a node,

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.660
that means they can control
all the pods running on a node.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.600
They can do things like abuse
compute at the node layer,

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.200
kill existing workloads,
and run arbitrary workloads.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.610
They also may try to attack
the master, the next layer up,

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:08.940
and do things like denial of
service attacks, for example.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.030
So next up, you've
got the master itself.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.190
That controls your
entire cluster.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.680
It controls workloads, where
they're scheduled, everything.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.030
So if an attacker compromises
your master, that's pretty bad.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.450
They can completely take
your environment offline.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.430
And short of killing
the entire cluster,

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:26.930
there's really not
much you're going

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.800
to be able to do to be able to
react to that type of attack.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.210
Finally, etcd.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.190
Again, like you're
Kubernetes hard drive,

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.620
that stores the
entire cluster state.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.050
So it's kind of like
stealing a hard drive.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.000
They're going to get
all your configs.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.580
The attacker could
create, modify, or destroy

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.440
your entire cluster,
and they may even

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:51.147
be able to recreate or model
your entire cluster elsewhere.

00:03:51.147 --> 00:03:53.230
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So let's
look at Google's shared

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.170
responsibility model and how
that applies to Google Cloud.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.840
So you might have heard this
term, shared responsibility

00:03:58.840 --> 00:03:59.935
model, before.

00:03:59.935 --> 00:04:02.560
It refers to how, in the cloud,
the cloud provider and the user

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.500
split the responsibility to
protect what you're running.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.500
So to be successful at
this, your cloud provider

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.110
has to be really clear about
which aspects of security

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.450
they're responsible for and
which aspects of security

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.797
you're responsible for.

00:04:14.797 --> 00:04:16.839
Generally, providers are
responsible for securing

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:18.964
infrastructure, and you're
responsible for securing

00:04:18.964 --> 00:04:20.050
workloads.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.040
Let's break this down
for Google Cloud.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.900
So as you can see
in the diagram here,

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:25.660
Google Cloud manages
the blue bits,

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.740
whereas the user
manages the green bits.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.190
For infrastructure as
a service, on the left,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.850
users are responsible for
things like network security,

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.700
the deployment itself, and web
app security, whereas Google

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.560
is responsible for just
the core infrastructure.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.510
For platform as a
service, in the middle,

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.435
Google takes on more
of the responsibility,

00:04:42.435 --> 00:04:44.560
including components like
authentication, identity,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.037
and operating the service.

00:04:46.037 --> 00:04:48.370
The user is still responsible
for deploying and securing

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:49.780
their application.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.810
And on the right, for
software as a service,

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.950
Google is also responsible
for these pieces.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.590
And so you, as the user, are
only responsible for protecting

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:00.412
your data and content using the
appropriate access policies.

00:05:00.412 --> 00:05:02.620
This breakdown is actually
available in a white paper

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.870
that we published late last
year on incident response,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.010
and I'll link to that
at the end of the talk.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.730
But looking at this,
where does GKE fall?

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.345
It's probably closest
to the middle.

00:05:11.345 --> 00:05:12.970
That is Google manages
a lot of the day

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.928
to day running of the
service, but you're still

00:05:14.928 --> 00:05:18.007
responsible for your specific
workloads that run on GKE.

00:05:18.007 --> 00:05:19.840
So let's quickly recap
what actually happens

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.632
at that bottom layer,
first-- the blue that

00:05:21.632 --> 00:05:24.010
goes across all of the bottom--
and how Google protects

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.440
those services on Google Cloud.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.270
So one of the key
security differentiators

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:30.880
for Google Cloud is our
core infrastructure.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.430
The infrastructure doesn't
rely on any single technology

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:34.570
to make it secure.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.330
Rather, we build security
through progressive layers

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.630
that deliver defense in depth.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.430
So starting from the
bottom up with hardware,

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.560
Google purpose builds
hardware in our data centers,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.800
from chip to network, in order
to ensure a secure supply

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.440
chain.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.720
Service deployment.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.783
We have a build, test,
and deployment pipeline

00:05:52.783 --> 00:05:55.200
that ensures that code running
on our servers and our data

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.912
centers is meant to be there,
so that any application

00:05:57.912 --> 00:05:59.370
binary that runs
the infrastructure

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.800
is purposely deployed.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.312
This lets us
enforce requirements

00:06:03.312 --> 00:06:04.770
like ensuring that
code is reviewed

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.330
by a second reviewer and other
such policies and requirements

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.620
we might have.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.530
Once deployed, we don't assume
any trust between services.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.630
They actually need to
authenticate to each other

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:16.450
in the environment.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.290
So we built this
infrastructure to be

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.990
multi-tenant from the get go.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.420
At the storage layer, data
storage and infrastructure

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.220
is automatically
encrypted at rest

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:29.040
and distributed for
availability and reliability.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.930
We also limited strict
data access controls

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.017
and robust logging features
at the storage layer,

00:06:33.017 --> 00:06:34.600
underlying several
of the applications

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.730
that you can use
on Google Cloud.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.730
Identity and access.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.980
All identities,
users, and services

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.630
are strongly authenticated.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.618
This authentication
for users, for example,

00:06:44.618 --> 00:06:46.410
using hardware second
factor, helps protect

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.390
against phishing attacks.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.010
Secure communication.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.460
Communications over the
internet to our cloud services

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.100
are encrypted, and
communications between services

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.170
internally are
authenticated and encrypted

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.560
when they go between
physical boundaries.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.277
And operational and
device security.

00:07:03.277 --> 00:07:04.860
The scale of our
infrastructure allows

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.150
us to absorb a lot of attacks,
including DDoS attacks.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.470
And our ops team
works 24/7 to respond

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.273
to threats and incidents.

00:07:13.273 --> 00:07:14.690
All of this is
explained in detail

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.360
in another white paper called
our "Infrastructure, Security,

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:18.818
Design White Paper,"
that I'll also

00:07:18.818 --> 00:07:20.430
link to at the end of the talk.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:21.620
But just note that
the way that Google

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:23.412
approaches a lot of
these things is unique.

00:07:23.412 --> 00:07:25.100
Anything that runs
on top of this stack,

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.740
including Google's workloads,
but also Google Cloud

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:30.200
workloads that our users want
to run, benefit from having

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.110
these underlying protections.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.960
So going to GKE, beyond
that base infrastructure,

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:37.850
let's look at Kubernetes.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.910
If you're running Kubernetes
yourself, for example, on GCE,

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:42.440
on prem, whatever
it happens to be,

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:44.607
you're responsible for
managing all of it, including

00:07:44.607 --> 00:07:47.630
the control plane and day
to day operational work.

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:51.047
We often see users start
with their own deployment,

00:07:51.047 --> 00:07:52.880
and then when it becomes
too much to handle,

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:54.800
migrate over to GKE.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.990
If instead, you use
GKE, Kubernetes engine

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.850
does a lot of the
heavy lifting for you.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.250
So for Kubernetes
Engine, at a high level,

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:05.447
the items on the left, Google
is responsible for protecting,

00:08:05.447 --> 00:08:07.280
in addition to the
underlying infrastructure

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.640
that I mentioned already,
the Kubernetes distribution.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:12.170
So Kubernetes Engine
makes available

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.970
the latest upstream
version of Kubernetes,

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:15.800
supporting several
minor versions.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.630
Providing updates to these
is Google's responsibility.

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:20.990
The control plane.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.520
In Kubernetes Engine,
Google manages

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.930
the control plane, which
includes the master

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:26.300
VMs, the API server, and
other components running

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:29.000
on those VMs, and
the etcd database.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.000
This includes upgrades, again,
including patching, scaling,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.730
and repairs all
backed by an SLO.

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.600
The operating systems
that your nodes run,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.450
such as Container Optimized
OS, or COS, and Ubuntu--

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.950
Kubernetes Engine promptly makes
any patches to these images

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:43.919
available.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:46.420
Note that this is the system
running on your nodes,

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:48.477
not the OS running
inside your containers.

00:08:48.477 --> 00:08:50.060
Think about containers
having two OSes

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:52.880
when you think about this.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.590
And the last piece
that Google manages

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.155
for you are Google Cloud
integrations for IAM,

00:08:57.155 --> 00:08:59.530
cloud audit logging, cloud
security command center, Cloud

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:02.270
KMS, Stackdriver, et cetera.

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.630
These enable controls available
for IS workloads across Google

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.090
Cloud directly in GKE.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.460
Conversely, the user is
responsible for protecting

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.790
the pieces on the right, the
nodes that run your workloads,

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.460
including VM images and
your configurations.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.830
You're responsible for any
extra software installed

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:21.170
in the nodes or any
configuration changes made

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.990
to the default. You're also
responsible for keeping

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:25.610
your nodes updated.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:28.160
Google provides hardened VM
images and configurations

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.230
by default, manages
the containers

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.570
that are necessary to
run GKE, and provides

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:33.980
patches for your OS.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.870
You're just responsible
for upgrading.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.720
If you use node auto upgrade,
it moves that responsibility

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.330
of upgrading the
nodes back to Google.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:43.610
And you're responsible for
your workloads, including

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:45.710
your application
code, Dockerfiles,

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.170
container images, content,
and your running containers

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:49.250
and pods.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:51.890
This includes leveraging GKE
features and other GCP features

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.190
to help protect what you're
running in containers.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.470
So let's go through a
couple of the components

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:59.728
here, the control plane,
the worker nodes, and then

00:09:59.728 --> 00:10:02.020
your workloads, to see how
we would protect each piece.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:04.550
So the control plane, first.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.040
Google, I just mentioned,
is responsible for securing

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.920
the control plane, which is a
component of Kubernetes that

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.743
manages how Kubernetes
communicates with the cluster

00:10:12.743 --> 00:10:14.910
and applies the user's
desired state to the cluster.

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:16.850
This is Google's responsibility.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.510
What does that mean?

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:22.250
Well, if you look at how
GKE's architected, in GKE,

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.900
the hosted control
plane components

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.720
run in a Google
owned GCP project.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:30.800
And they control resources that
are in a user's GCP project.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.870
Each customer's control
plane components

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:36.838
run on GCE instances, and these
instances are single tenants,

00:10:36.838 --> 00:10:38.630
meaning that each
instance runs the control

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:42.000
plane and its components
for only one customer.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.330
So what's actually in
that control plane?

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.670
A couple of different pieces.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.090
First, the master VM.

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.870
It's a set of virtual machines
that runs master components.

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:53.530
Like I just mentioned, these
are single tenant GCE VMs

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:55.400
in a Google owned project.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:58.430
Google uses Container
Optimized OS, or COS, for these

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:02.420
and manages the health of these
VMs, including updating the OS.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.660
The API server, scheduler,
controller manager,

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:08.900
and other controllers run
on top of these master VMs.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.510
These are the typical functions
of your Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:14.630
After all, the API that you're
interacting with when use GKE

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:16.700
is the same as a
normal Kubernetes API

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.200
that you get by
running it yourself.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.200
It just happens to
be managed by Google.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.895
The control plane
also manages etcd,

00:11:22.895 --> 00:11:24.770
which is the database
behind Kubernetes which

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.370
maintains state.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:30.020
Just like the rest of GCP,
content stored in etcd and GKE

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:33.140
is encrypted at the file
system layer by default.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:35.450
So you don't have to do
anything to get that.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:37.437
Etcd only listens
on two TCP ports,

00:11:37.437 --> 00:11:39.020
one to communicate
with the Kubernetes

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.970
API and the other for server
to server communication.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.780
When transmitting traffic from
one entity server to another,

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:50.110
these communications are
protected using mutual TLS.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:53.170
Next piece, the control plane
also runs the cluster CA

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.230
and manages the cluster's
root of trust key material.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:59.450
Each cluster has its own
root certificate authority.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:03.220
The API server and kubelets rely
on Kubernetes cluster root CA

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:05.080
for trust.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:08.350
Each cluster runs its own CA, so
that if one cluster's CA would

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:12.160
be compromised, no other
cluster's CA would be affected.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.190
An internal Google service
manages route keys for the CA,

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:19.620
and these are privately held by
Google outside of the cluster.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.790
IAM authentication
and authorization

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:23.512
is handled by the
master, as well.

00:12:23.512 --> 00:12:25.720
This works the same way for
the Kubernetes API server

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.930
and etcd, as it's done for
other Google Cloud Services.

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:32.110
And lastly, the master includes
the audit logging configuration

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:33.760
for the master components.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:36.820
This provides a detailed record,
available in Stackdriver,

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:41.170
of calls that are made to
the Kubernetes API server.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:42.560
That was quite a lot.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.550
So the goal here and throughout
Kubernetes Engine, as you'll

00:12:45.550 --> 00:12:48.700
see, is to give you strong
security everywhere, control

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:50.682
where you need it, and
sane defaults in case

00:12:50.682 --> 00:12:52.390
you never quite get
around to configuring

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:53.960
that thing yourself.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:55.972
So keeping the goal of
sane defaults in mind,

00:12:55.972 --> 00:12:57.430
we've been making
hardening changes

00:12:57.430 --> 00:12:59.360
to the control plane over time.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:01.450
So let's see what's
actually changed, recently.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:07.670
Let's look at the change in
some of the recent GKE releases.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:11.360
First, in 1.7, the
Kubernetes dashboard in GKE

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:14.870
had its highly privileged
admin access removed.

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:18.002
You might have heard of a couple
of public hacks of Kubernetes,

00:13:18.002 --> 00:13:19.460
in which case,
you've probably seen

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:21.440
the dashboard
prominently featured.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.930
Tesla, Aviva, Weight
Watchers were all

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:27.260
affected in late
2017 and early 2018,

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.960
where they had their dashboards
deployed on the public web,

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.690
not password protected, and
had privileged cloud service

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:34.040
account credentials in there.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:37.250
Attackers found the dashboards,
went in, took the credentials,

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.750
logged into to
the cloud service,

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.480
and started
cryptocurrency mining.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:43.790
So on GKE, we had actually
made this hardening change

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:46.670
before these attacks occurred.

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:51.020
In 1.8, GKE enabled role-based
access controller, RBAC,

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.300
by default in the V1 API.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:56.210
RBAC is a method of
restricting access to resources

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:58.430
based on roles and permissions.

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:01.220
In Kubernetes, it's implemented
to allow you control access

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:04.520
to pods, deployments, config
maps, jobs, basically,

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.160
anything you can think of.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:10.250
It's meant to replace
the prior authentication

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:12.490
method of attribute
based access control

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:15.200
and provides a more intuitive
way of handling permissions.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:16.910
By using RBAC, you
can more easily

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.160
follow the principle of
least privileged to set rules

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:23.730
with minimum permissions to
resources inside your cluster.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.690
In 1.8.3, GKE enabled Cloud
Audit Logging with the default

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:32.020
Kubernetes policy, so that both
your GCP API and the Kubernetes

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:34.080
API audit logs are
written to Stackdriver

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.640
for admin activities.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:39.450
This is to easily go back
and see who did what and when

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.520
in your cluster.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.812
In 1.10, in response to the
attacks that I mentioned

00:14:43.812 --> 00:14:46.020
earlier that were happening
in the wild against Tesla

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:47.334
and others, we--

00:14:47.334 --> 00:14:51.138
I just lost my mic.

00:14:51.138 --> 00:14:51.930
I just lost my mic.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:52.770
OK, good.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.020
In response to the attack,
which occurred in the wild,

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.187
against Tesla and others,
we disabled the Kubernetes

00:14:57.187 --> 00:14:59.340
dashboard by default in GKE.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:00.930
Instead, you can use
the Cloud Console

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:05.400
to have the same functionality
to monitor your clusters.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.570
The next bit requires a
little bit of explanation.

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:10.680
So Kubernetes offers several
authentication methods.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.900
In GKE, the supported methods
are OpenID connect tokens,

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:17.470
x509 client certificates,
and static passwords.

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:20.490
GKE manages
authentication for G Cloud

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.990
by using the OpenID
Connect Token method,

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.990
setting up the Kubernetes
configuration for you,

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.900
getting an access token,
and keeping it up to date.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.440
The other
authentication methods,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:33.570
x509 certs and static passwords,
present a wider surface

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:34.680
of attack.

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:38.070
So basic OS has never
really been a best practice.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:41.970
And x509 certs in Kubernetes
are poorly auditable.

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:44.220
So unless your application
specifically needs these,

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:46.720
you shouldn't be enabling
these in your cluster.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.888
So starting in 1.12,
GKE no longer generates

00:15:49.888 --> 00:15:51.430
these credentials
for you by default,

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:54.110
when you create a new cluster.

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:57.020
And also in 1.12,
GKE removed access

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:01.130
to the GCE legacy metadata
server APIs for your cluster.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:03.050
Some practical attacks
that were happening

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:04.970
in the wild against
Kubernetes rely

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:06.680
on access to the
VMs metadata server

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:08.630
to extract the
node's credentials.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:11.210
Notably, Shopify, last
year, had a bug bounty,

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:13.370
where a researcher
accessed this metadata

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:16.110
and had broader access to
the cluster than expected.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.037
So we removed that by default.

00:16:18.037 --> 00:16:20.120
Note that all of these
hardening efforts that I've

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:23.750
been talking about change the
defaults for new clusters.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:25.220
We can't do this
for older clusters

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:26.860
to allow for backwards
compatibility.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.360
So if you have an older cluster
that you've been continually

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:31.570
upgrading, please go in
and make sure to change

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:32.945
some of these
defaults yourselves

00:16:32.945 --> 00:16:37.190
to get the leading
edge security.

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.355
So critically,
though, Google is also

00:16:39.355 --> 00:16:40.730
responsible for
patching anything

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.210
running in the control plane.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.960
We have a high bar
for security, but bugs

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:47.382
do occur in open source
projects and things that

00:16:47.382 --> 00:16:48.590
are running in your clusters.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:51.020
So we want make sure to
properly patch these.

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:53.700
Google automatically
upgrades the master VMs,

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.700
but you're responsible
for upgrading your nodes.

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:58.370
And I'll talk more about auto
node upgrade in a second.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:02.060
So since there are so many
different pieces here,

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.980
let's quickly go over what
this looks like in practice.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:07.730
It depends where the
vulnerability is.

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:11.780
If the vulnerability is in the
kernel or an operating system,

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:14.359
Google will apply the patch
to affected components,

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:16.460
including obtaining
and applying the patch

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:20.300
to host images for
Kubernetes, COS, and Ubuntu.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:23.060
Spectre, and Meltdown,
and L1 TF are examples

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.650
of such vulnerabilities.

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:27.457
If the vulnerabiliy
is in Kubernetes,

00:17:27.457 --> 00:17:29.540
Google is involved in
Kubernetes' product security

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:31.730
team and often helps
develop and test patches

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:33.770
for Kubernetes vulnerabilities.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:36.350
Since GKE is an
official distribution,

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:38.090
Google receives
the patch as part

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:40.960
of the private
distributors list.

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:44.260
If the vuln is in a component
used in GKE's default

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:47.230
configuration, for example,
Calico's container network

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:50.260
interface, CNI, or an
etcd, then in this case,

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:53.020
Google may receive a patch
from upstream Kubernetes

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:54.970
from a partner or from
the distributor list

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:57.510
of another open source project.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:00.000
And lastly, if the
vuln is in GKE itself,

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.500
for example, if it's discovered
through Google Vulnerability

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:05.650
Rewards Program, then Google
is responsible for developing

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.100
and applying the fix.

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.740
In all of these cases,
Google makes the patches

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.510
available as part of general
Kubernetes Engine releases,

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:17.250
which are the patch releases and
bug fixes as soon as possible,

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.130
given the level of
risk, embargo time,

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:22.200
and any other
contextual factors.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.510
Where the vulnerability
is severe enough or user

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.900
action is required, we
publish a security bulletin

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.650
detailing this information
on our website.

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:33.760
So that was the control plane.

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:36.530
Now, let's look at the
nodes-- the worker nodes.

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:39.590
For your nodes, Google
does most of the hard work

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:41.445
by providing the
components that you need

00:18:41.445 --> 00:18:43.070
and the patches that
you need for those

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:44.820
when there's a security issue.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.400
But it's still
your responsibility

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:50.420
to upgrade in order to
reap those benefits.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.790
In Kubernetes, your
worker nodes are

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:55.010
where you run your workloads.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:58.940
Your worker nodes in GKE consist
of a few different surfaces

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:01.280
that need to be
protected, including

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:04.820
the node OS, the container
runtime, Kubernetes

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:07.820
components like the
kubelet and kube-proxy,

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:11.810
and Google system containers
for monitoring and logging.

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:13.550
Google provides all
of these components

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:16.400
and, where necessary, you decide
what you want for your nodes.

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:18.800
So you get to choose the
operating system between COS

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:22.370
and Ubuntu, and you choose
a runtime that you'd like.

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:25.040
Separate containers run
for Kubernetes components,

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:27.830
like kube-proxy and
kube DNS, and Google

00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:30.450
specific add-ons to provide
logging, monitoring,

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:31.700
and other services.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.340
Google's responsible
for those containers.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:37.250
In all of these cases, Google
is responsible for patching

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:39.860
these components, but you
are responsible for deciding

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:42.440
when to upgrade to
apply those patches.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:45.320
So for example, a patch
to COS, to container D,

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:48.170
or Kubernetes would
all be provided by GKE,

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:52.310
but the user has to
upgrade to apply them.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:54.900
Let's talk a little bit
more about the OS choice.

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:57.538
GKE provides two options
for your node OS.

00:19:57.538 --> 00:19:59.330
Again, this is the OS
running on your node,

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.940
not the OS running
inside of your container.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:06.200
You can use Container Optimized
OS, or COS, and Ubuntu.

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:08.540
As mentioned earlier, GKE's
control plane uses COS.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:11.540
So you're deciding what you want
to run on your worker nodes.

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:13.130
So why should you consider COS?

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:16.160
Well, it has a couple of
nice security properties.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:18.453
COS is based off of Chromium OS.

00:20:18.453 --> 00:20:20.120
Google maintains all
of these components

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.850
as it is able to rebuild from
source if a new vulnerability

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:24.860
is discovered and
needs to be patched,

00:20:24.860 --> 00:20:27.290
often allowing us to make
a patch available sooner

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:30.330
than if using a third
party distribution.

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.710
COS is minimal and has a
smaller attack surface.

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:36.210
COS is purpose built
to run containers

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:38.750
and so has a smaller
footprint than other OSes.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:41.660
This means less code, less
complexity, and therefore

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:42.920
less patching.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.600
There's less stuff
to patch if you never

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:47.100
had the component
in the first place.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:50.417
COS is hardened with
good security defaults.

00:20:50.417 --> 00:20:51.750
I really want to emphasize this.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:54.250
This is very critical for some
of the recent vulnerabilities

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:55.430
we've had, like runC.

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:59.420
These were preventable because
of COS' security properties.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:03.560
A firewall restricts all TCP UDP
traffic, except SSH on Port 22.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.175
And load pin prevents kernel
modules from being installed.

00:21:06.175 --> 00:21:09.560
A read only root file
system and verified boot

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.200
makes persistence for
attackers more difficult.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.348
COS also allows security
features like Satcom

00:21:14.348 --> 00:21:15.890
to further constrain
your application

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:19.490
to its normal behavior and
EVPF for third party security

00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:21.410
products to monitor.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:24.030
Lastly, COS offers
automatic updates.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.760
So in GKE, we
provide COS updates

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:28.867
on a weekly basis as part of
the normal release process.

00:21:28.867 --> 00:21:30.950
Your nodes will automatically
download the updates

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:34.160
in the background and then
upgrade when you reboot.

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:36.148
So we've been talking
about nodes and how

00:21:36.148 --> 00:21:38.690
your primary responsibility for
these nodes, to protect them,

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:40.048
is to update them.

00:21:40.048 --> 00:21:41.840
To make sure you benefit
from the hard work

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:46.340
that Google does, to patch the
pieces running on your nodes.

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:50.030
What if even that, you could
just hand over to Google?

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.790
And that's exactly what
node auto upgrade is.

00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:53.360
It's funny.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:55.040
I think we talk a lot
about node auto upgrade

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:57.582
as being a reliability feature,
but I entirely think about it

00:21:57.582 --> 00:21:59.660
as being a security feature.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:01.310
Node upgrades help
keep your nodes up

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:04.130
to date with the latest,
stable version of Kubernetes.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:06.385
This means you stay up to
date with feature changes,

00:22:06.385 --> 00:22:07.760
but it also means
that Google can

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:11.000
apply a security patch to
your nodes when it's critical.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.710
Node auto upgrade
is available for COS

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:14.670
and also, more
recently, for Ubuntu.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.337
So you have no reason
not to turn it on.

00:22:16.337 --> 00:22:18.295
So I mentioned that node
auto upgrade keeps you

00:22:18.295 --> 00:22:19.790
on the latest, stable patch.

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:22.670
How does this work if there's
a newly disclosed security

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:24.450
vulnerability?

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:26.930
Well, a patch with
a vulnerability fix

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.210
is rolled into the
first possible release

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:31.150
after public disclosure.

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:32.985
Often, this is the
patch that's released

00:22:32.985 --> 00:22:34.610
the day of a disclosure,
but then takes

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:36.735
multiple days to actually
roll out and be available

00:22:36.735 --> 00:22:37.730
everywhere.

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:41.210
Then several weeks later, once
a release is considered stable,

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:43.160
it's applied to the whole fleet.

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.770
For some security patches where
it's politically critical,

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:48.300
this is actually
done much sooner.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.365
So why would you not
turn on auto grade?

00:22:50.365 --> 00:22:52.490
Well, you might need deeper
validation of an update

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:55.580
yourself or want additional
control and predictability

00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:57.980
for your SRE team to be on call.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.770
If this is the case, consider
turning on auto upgrade

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:03.890
with a maintenance window,
so that it will only apply it

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:05.507
during that time period.

00:23:05.507 --> 00:23:07.340
We also see users turn
off note auto upgrade

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:09.740
around major launches or
events like Black Friday,

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:14.360
where changes are particularly
sensitive to their environment.

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:17.420
Node auto upgrade works
by creating a new node

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:19.778
and moving the workload
over to that node.

00:23:19.778 --> 00:23:21.320
Let me quickly
explain how surge auto

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:23.112
upgrade works, which
are newer developments

00:23:23.112 --> 00:23:24.230
to node auto upgrade.

00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:26.540
First, we create a new surge VM.

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:29.690
And once it's up and running,
create the node on that VM.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:32.148
That takes a few minutes.

00:23:32.148 --> 00:23:33.815
Once the new node is
ready, the old node

00:23:33.815 --> 00:23:35.690
is drained of the workload.

00:23:35.690 --> 00:23:37.400
And once it's fully
drained, the workload

00:23:37.400 --> 00:23:39.080
resumes on the new node.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.282
Draining the old node
takes a few seconds.

00:23:41.282 --> 00:23:43.490
And with the workload up
and running on the new node,

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:46.803
we can now delete the old VM.

00:23:46.803 --> 00:23:48.470
So we heard in the
keynote this morning,

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:49.840
talk about live migration.

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:52.250
This is applying the same
concept for your workloads,

00:23:52.250 --> 00:23:54.280
for your nodes running
in your environment.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:56.120
Live migration also
underlies all of GKE,

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:59.720
so you get that
automatically, anyways.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:01.610
And because of the
security benefits and ease

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:03.133
of maintenance,
we're slowly moving

00:24:03.133 --> 00:24:04.550
to making auto
upgrade the default

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:06.530
for new clusters in GKE.

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:08.150
So for clusters
created in the UI

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:12.150
since about October, auto
upgrade is checked by default.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:14.030
For clusters created
with G Cloud,

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:16.460
starting for G
Cloud beta in 1.13,

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.650
you no longer have to pass
the enable auto upgrade

00:24:18.650 --> 00:24:22.370
flag if you don't want to.

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:23.713
I'm sorry, to enable this.

00:24:23.713 --> 00:24:25.130
If you don't want
to use this, you

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:28.590
can pass the dash dash no
enable auto upgrade flag.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.590
And it's not yet the
default in the API, though.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:32.360
So be careful if
you're an opt in,

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:33.943
if you're primarily
using the API.

00:24:33.943 --> 00:24:35.360
In all of these
cases, though, you

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:40.380
can still opt out by unchecking
a box or passing a flag.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:40.880
Great.

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:44.932
Let's talk about
workload security.

00:24:44.932 --> 00:24:47.140
JESSE ENDAHL: So what we've
been talking about so far

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:50.310
is the underlying infrastructure
that runs your workloads.

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:52.000
But of course, you
still have to worry

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:55.000
about the workloads themselves,
and application security,

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:58.330
and other aspects
of your workloads.

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:01.420
Protecting that is your
responsibility, not Google's.

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:03.580
So when we talk about
hardening your workloads,

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:06.460
there's really two places you
need to look to secure them--

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:09.430
your Kubernetes configs that
pertain to your workloads

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:12.620
and, obviously, the
workloads themselves.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:15.880
So last year, Maya and
I presented a roadmap

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:19.480
for securing your organization
on Kubernetes as it matures.

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:23.350
Some of the things are
handled automatically by GKE,

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:25.000
and some aren't.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:29.398
Last year, specifically in
terms of Kubernetes configs,

00:25:29.398 --> 00:25:31.690
we talked about a few of
these things, not all of them.

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:34.300
Things like using
a network policy,

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:37.450
using namespaces, and setting
a pod security policy.

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:39.940
And one that we didn't go
into in detail last year

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:40.930
is protecting secrets.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:43.492
So we'll talk a little bit
more about that, today.

00:25:43.492 --> 00:25:44.950
In terms of additional
controls you

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:46.600
can put in place for
your application,

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:48.790
you can verify
binaries, scan images

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:51.700
for known vulnerabilities,
and use sandboxing.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:53.312
To learn more about
all this stuff,

00:25:53.312 --> 00:25:54.770
check out that talk
from last year.

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:56.920
It's called Kubernetes
for enterprise security

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:58.000
requirements.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.460
And for an up to date
list of best practices,

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:03.340
we recommend checking out
the "Hardening Your Cluster

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:04.990
Security" guide.

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:06.925
This year, we wanted
to focus on a few

00:26:06.925 --> 00:26:09.092
of those areas we didn't
get to dive into last year,

00:26:09.092 --> 00:26:11.830
like I mentioned, such
as protecting secrets.

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:14.020
So what is a secret?

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:15.970
A secret can be a lot
of different things.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:18.040
Technically, it's
any sensitive data

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:19.870
that your application
needs either at

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:22.480
build time or runtime.

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:26.103
Some common examples are
TLS private keys, API keys,

00:26:26.103 --> 00:26:27.520
username and
password combinations

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:30.280
to access your
database, for example,

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:32.590
or external services like
your payments processor,

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:35.410
such as a stripe API key.

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.870
Things that are not
considered secrets

00:26:37.870 --> 00:26:41.120
are your code and general
configuration files.

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:44.100
So let's say you're using
Nginx as your web server.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:46.370
The Nginx config
file is not a secret,

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:49.840
but the TLS private key that's
referenced by that config

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:51.047
is a secret.

00:26:51.047 --> 00:26:52.630
It's also worth
calling out that there

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:57.267
is sensitive data or personally
identifiable information, PII.

00:26:57.267 --> 00:26:59.600
These things are not considered
quote unquote "secrets,"

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:01.720
even though they're
obviously sensitive data.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.473
And you still want to protect
that sensitive data or PII,

00:27:04.473 --> 00:27:05.890
but generally, you
want to do that

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:08.440
by encrypting it before it's
written to the database,

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:09.440
for example.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:12.400
And you wouldn't typically store
that type of sensitive data

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:16.360
directly inside your
secrets management system.

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:19.230
So by default in
Kubernetes, secrets

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:21.810
are stored in that
storage layer in etcd,

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:24.060
but they have no
additional protection.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.310
They're protected in the
same way as the rest of etcd.

00:27:27.310 --> 00:27:30.330
So a pod can access secrets
via the file system,

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:33.740
either as an environment
variable or via an API call.

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:36.540
And effectively, all nodes
can access all secrets,

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:39.390
and users with access to
etcd can exfiltrate secrets

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:41.190
in plaintext.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.860
Starting in Kubernetes
1.7, Kubernetes

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.230
offers application
layer encryption

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:48.360
for differential
protection of secrets.

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:50.130
Using this feature,
your secrets are

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:51.900
encrypted with a
key that's specified

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.390
in the local etcd secret config.

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:56.790
But this option is
actually not recommended,

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:59.040
because it doesn't actually
improve your security.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:00.870
If you think about
your threat model,

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:02.850
it's actually the same
exact threat model

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:05.730
as it was before, since the
critical data that an attacker

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:10.170
would access is being stored
in the same place as the key.

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:12.630
So it doesn't really help you.

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:16.380
Starting in Kubernetes 1.10,
Kubernetes offers something

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:20.770
called envelope encryption of
secrets using a KMS provider.

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:23.052
We're not going to go into
a lot of detail, here.

00:28:23.052 --> 00:28:24.510
But a quick summary
of how it works

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:28.020
is that there are
two keys and then

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:29.940
the data that's actually
being encrypted.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:32.580
So in this case, the data
being encrypted, obviously,

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:36.450
is the Kubernetes secrets
that are stored with an etcd.

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:40.440
Key number one is called the
key encryption key, or KEK.

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:44.190
The only purpose of that key
is to encrypt the second key.

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:46.510
The second key is called
the data encryption key.

00:28:46.510 --> 00:28:48.660
And as you can probably
guess, its only purpose

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:52.420
is to encrypt the data.

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.830
For more details on how
all this stuff works,

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:57.620
check out the talk by Seth
Vargo and Alex Chernokovski

00:28:57.620 --> 00:28:59.620
called "Securing
Kubernetes Secrets."

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:00.550
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: It's
worth pointing out

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:03.190
that on Google Cloud, we've
used this functionality that's

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:05.650
from Kubernetes
natively into GKE.

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:07.420
So we support this
with a feature

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:09.280
called application
layer secrets encryption

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:10.960
for GKE, which is in beta.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:13.720
You can use a key in Cloud KMS
to encrypt the data encryption

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:15.623
key that encrypts
your secrets in GKE.

00:29:15.623 --> 00:29:17.290
It addresses the
encryption requirements

00:29:17.290 --> 00:29:19.000
that a lot of users
have for those secrets

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:20.583
and provides that
separation of duties

00:29:20.583 --> 00:29:22.960
between who manages the
secrets in your clusters

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:26.077
and who manages keys.

00:29:26.077 --> 00:29:27.910
JESSE ENDAHL: So at
Fleetsmith, we currently

00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:30.345
use Hashicorp Vault
for secrets management.

00:29:30.345 --> 00:29:33.340
This is because we
currently run what

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:37.360
I call a hybrid environment,
where some of our application

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:39.970
runs in VMs and some
runs in containers.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:42.940
So because of that,
we rely on something

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:46.600
called the GCP authentication
plugin for Hashicorp Vault

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:49.780
to bootstrap secrets
onto new instances.

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:53.334
If you're also running that
kind of hybrid structure--

00:29:53.334 --> 00:29:55.570
lost the mic.

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:56.070
Hello?

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:57.340
Testing.

00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:59.200
So if you're also
running a hybrid type

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:02.260
of cloud infrastructure, where
you have both instances or VMs,

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:05.350
as well as Kubernetes pods
or collections of containers,

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:09.820
then Vault is actually a
great solution for that.

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:11.780
You do have that
challenge, though,

00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:14.490
of how do you safely
bootstrap those secrets

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:15.970
onto new instances and pods.

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:17.720
And that's where things
really get tricky.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:20.290
So to make sure you're safely
bootstrapping those secrets,

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:25.180
you want to use the GCP off
method for your VMs with Vault,

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.550
and you want to use the
Kubernetes off method

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:31.060
to bootstrap secrets onto
your pods with Kubernetes.

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:33.010
And of course,
regardless, you want

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:36.040
to be really careful with
your ACLs to make sure

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:39.130
that you're scoping the
access to your secrets

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:41.830
to only the pods or only the
VMs that actually need access

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:43.800
to those secrets.

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:46.300
I will actually say, though,
if you're starting fresh today,

00:30:46.300 --> 00:30:48.430
and you don't have
legacy reasons

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:50.770
for rolling your own
Kubernetes cluster,

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:53.230
I highly recommend
just going with GKE

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:55.810
and using encrypted secrets.

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:57.860
If you want to learn
more info on that topic,

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:00.340
check out the talk,
"Key-less Entry.

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:03.880
Securely Accessing GCP
Services From Kubernetes."

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:06.280
That talk is tomorrow,
Wednesday, April 10th,

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:09.437
from 2:10 to 3:00 PM.

00:31:09.437 --> 00:31:10.520
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Awesome.

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:13.700
So other ways we can help
with your workload security,

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:16.700
Google offers a few products and
features to make this easier.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:19.123
First, managed base images
and distroless images.

00:31:19.123 --> 00:31:21.290
So I was talking earlier a
lot about the node image,

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:22.310
the node OS.

00:31:22.310 --> 00:31:24.440
This is about what's running
inside your container.

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:26.540
You're going to have a
base image that you build

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:28.120
your container on top
of, and you build it

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:29.203
into an application image.

00:31:29.203 --> 00:31:31.830
And that's actually
what you deploy.

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:35.330
So your container image is built
by taking that operating system

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:37.670
base image and
adding the libraries,

00:31:37.670 --> 00:31:40.820
the packages, the binaries
that you actually need.

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:43.400
Managed based images
are one option.

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:45.470
These are images that
are patched for security

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:47.480
vulnerabilities
automatically by Google

00:31:47.480 --> 00:31:50.750
with the most recently
available patches from upstream.

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:52.493
Google maintains
several base images

00:31:52.493 --> 00:31:53.910
for building its
own applications,

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:56.645
including things
like App Engine.

00:31:56.645 --> 00:31:59.270
So managed based images actually
have a couple of nice security

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:00.645
properties as well
that make them

00:32:00.645 --> 00:32:03.230
a desirable choice
for your applications.

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:05.480
Specifically, they're
regularly scanned

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:08.230
for known vulnerabilities
from the CVE database.

00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:10.550
This scanning is done using
the same functionality

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:12.470
as Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning,

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:13.790
if you've seen that before.

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:17.000
And where a patch for
these exists, it's applied.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:20.210
They're built reproducibly, so
that there is a verifiable path

00:32:20.210 --> 00:32:22.372
from source code to the binary.

00:32:22.372 --> 00:32:24.080
That means that the
image can be verified

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:26.580
to ensure that no flaws have
been introduced by comparing it

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:28.070
to source.

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:29.900
And lastly, they're
stored on Google Cloud,

00:32:29.900 --> 00:32:31.610
so you can pull them directly
from your environment

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:33.440
without having to
traverse networks.

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:36.350
You can pull these images
using private Google access.

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:38.870
Managed base images are
available for Debian, Ubuntu,

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:40.572
and CentOS.

00:32:40.572 --> 00:32:42.530
Another option, an
alternative to managed based

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:44.910
images are distroless images.

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:46.940
These images only
contain your application

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:49.580
and it's runtime
dependencies, greatly reducing

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:51.582
the potential surface of attack.

00:32:51.582 --> 00:32:53.540
A package with a newly
discovered vulnerability

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:55.490
can't affect you if
you don't actually have

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:57.110
the package in the first place.

00:32:57.110 --> 00:33:00.230
This is how you become
more secure, less stuff.

00:33:00.230 --> 00:33:03.405
Distroless images remove
package managers, shells,

00:33:03.405 --> 00:33:05.780
or other programs that you
might find in a standard Linux

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:08.120
distribution, so that you're
focusing on what's actually

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:11.450
important, which is dealing
with the vulnerability

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:13.400
scan results that you
have and leaving you

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:15.480
with less to maintain.

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:18.410
Distroless images are language
focused and available for many

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:21.680
languages, including Java,
Python, Node.js, .NET,

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:26.180
and more, as well as a static
image for statically compiled

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:28.730
languages like Go, Rest, and D.

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:31.130
Both distroless
images and managed

00:33:31.130 --> 00:33:33.770
based images are good
choices for your containers.

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:36.410
It really depends on what
works in your environment.

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:38.900
If you need a full Linux
distribution, including

00:33:38.900 --> 00:33:41.480
features like a package
manager or a shell,

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:44.175
then managed based
images are a good choice.

00:33:44.175 --> 00:33:45.800
If you want the most
locked down option

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:51.270
possible, then distroless
images might be better for you.

00:33:51.270 --> 00:33:52.670
So now that you
have a base image

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:54.500
and you've actually built
your container image,

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:57.042
you have something that you're
storing in your image registry

00:33:57.042 --> 00:33:58.440
that you're going to deploy.

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:00.230
So for those application
images, you'll

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:03.950
also want to avoid including any
known vulnerabilities in those.

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:06.710
To assess that in Google
Container Registry,

00:34:06.710 --> 00:34:08.780
you can use
vulnerability scanning.

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:10.940
GCR vulnerability
scanning, when enabled,

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:14.630
scans all the images in your
private registry for known CVEs

00:34:14.630 --> 00:34:16.280
from the CVE database.

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:18.350
This is displayed
in your registry

00:34:18.350 --> 00:34:20.389
with info as to whether
a fix is available

00:34:20.389 --> 00:34:22.431
and the severity of the
vulnerability, et cetera,

00:34:22.431 --> 00:34:24.530
so that you can
actually take action.

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:26.630
This scan is conducted
on a new image when

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:30.500
it's added to the registry, as
well as, for existing images,

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:32.840
when a new vulnerability
is discovered and added

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:35.159
to the database.

00:34:35.159 --> 00:34:37.790
GCR vulnerability scanning is
available for Debian, Ubuntu,

00:34:37.790 --> 00:34:41.650
CentOS, and Alpine images.

00:34:41.650 --> 00:34:44.530
And at the end of the day, if
you're responding to something

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:45.947
happening in your
environment, you

00:34:45.947 --> 00:34:48.040
can look up at Cloud
Security Scanner.

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:49.630
Sorry, that made no sense.

00:34:49.630 --> 00:34:52.330
You can also use Cloud Security
Scanner to scan for common web

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:53.830
application vulnerabilities.

00:34:56.712 --> 00:34:58.420
Like for other services,
security scanner

00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:01.540
can be used on public facing
applications hosted on GKE.

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:05.260
It scans for cross-eyed
scripting, mixed content,

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:07.750
outdated libraries, clear text
passwords, and other common,

00:35:07.750 --> 00:35:11.117
like OS top 10 like vulns.

00:35:11.117 --> 00:35:13.450
So now, we've protected our
infrastructure-- the control

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:16.930
plane, the worker nodes, and
your workloads themselves.

00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:21.130
And unfortunately, in security,
bad things still happen.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:24.550
So what do you do when you
have to respond to a security

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:25.247
incident?

00:35:25.247 --> 00:35:27.580
Now, it's also worth noting
I'm making a difference here

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.010
between incidents
and vulnerabilities.

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.760
A new vulnerability,
if it's not currently

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:33.260
being exploited in
your environment,

00:35:33.260 --> 00:35:34.090
is not an incident.

00:35:34.090 --> 00:35:36.465
You should have that handled
by your normal vulnerability

00:35:36.465 --> 00:35:39.400
assessment response team.

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:42.280
So responding to attacks
on Google's infrastructure

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:44.920
is Google's responsibility,
whereas responding

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:48.590
to attacks on user workloads
is the user's responsibility.

00:35:48.590 --> 00:35:50.590
Basically, we're each
responsible for responding

00:35:50.590 --> 00:35:53.200
to attacks on pieces
that we protect.

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:54.560
That makes sense.

00:35:54.560 --> 00:35:56.450
So how do you handle
incident response?

00:35:59.410 --> 00:36:03.160
JESSE ENDAHL: So before
we answer that question,

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:05.230
we have to define what
does that actually mean,

00:36:05.230 --> 00:36:09.100
being responsible for incident
response on your workloads.

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:11.110
Well, it really
means, to start, you

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:14.120
need to understand the
incident response lifecycle.

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:16.120
And so let's just quickly
go over those stages.

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:17.920
That means being
able to identify when

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:19.750
an incident actually occurs.

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:21.400
Establishing a runbook
for what happens

00:36:21.400 --> 00:36:23.320
once an incident is declared.

00:36:23.320 --> 00:36:25.235
Staffing your own
response team, who

00:36:25.235 --> 00:36:26.860
will actually be
following that runbook

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:28.420
in the case of an incident.

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:30.070
Understanding the
scope of the incident

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:31.240
by conducting forensics.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:33.730
And finally, notifying
any affected users,

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:36.760
if that's applicable, and
developing and executing

00:36:36.760 --> 00:36:37.720
on a remediation plan.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:42.470
So if you're responding
to an incident,

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:43.930
and you're on Google
Cloud, there's

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:45.840
a few tools that you
can leverage with GKE

00:36:45.840 --> 00:36:49.570
to help conduct your
own incident response.

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:52.590
First up is absolutely critical.

00:36:52.590 --> 00:36:55.450
You need to make sure you have
a centralized log aggregation

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:56.860
system set up.

00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:59.725
On Google Cloud, Stackdriver
could help with this.

00:36:59.725 --> 00:37:01.600
It comes prepackaged
with common queries that

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:03.400
are helpful for
addressing incidents,

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:05.230
specifically, that occur on GKE.

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:08.113
So for example, in the case
of a cluster compromise,

00:37:08.113 --> 00:37:10.030
you might want to quickly
check what resources

00:37:10.030 --> 00:37:12.520
were accessed by the attacker.

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:15.160
And you can leverage those types
of built in queries instead

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:17.380
of spending valuable minutes
on writing those queries

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:19.800
from scratch yourself.

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:22.780
And next, Stackdriver Incident
Response and Management

00:37:22.780 --> 00:37:24.100
is a new product from Google.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:25.300
That's in alpha.

00:37:25.300 --> 00:37:27.820
It's a set of new tooling
that they're specifically

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:30.160
developing to help
you reduce time

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:32.500
to mitigation in an
incident response

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:35.710
scenario on Google Cloud.

00:37:35.710 --> 00:37:38.300
If you're interested in more
material on incident response,

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:40.060
including how Google
themselves uses

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:43.270
a really awesome open
source tool called Gur,

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:47.020
internally, I highly recommend
a talk from last year's Google

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:51.112
Cloud Next called,
"Cloud Forensics 101."

00:37:51.112 --> 00:37:52.570
And if you're a
startup that's just

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:55.140
getting started with
incident response,

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:57.070
there's a really
great starting point

00:37:57.070 --> 00:38:01.360
in the form of a blog post by
someone named Brian McKehan.

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:04.540
It's titled, "An Incident
Response Plan For Startups,"

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:08.920
and you can find the link
at the end of this talk.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:10.390
Finally, I do want to call out--

00:38:10.390 --> 00:38:11.810
Brian's not a Googler.

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:14.347
He's formally a director of
incident response at Facebook,

00:38:14.347 --> 00:38:16.180
as well as director of
security at CoinBase.

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:19.060
And he currently has
his own consulting firm

00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:22.810
called R10N Security.

00:38:22.810 --> 00:38:24.560
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So
if you are responding

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:26.435
to an incident on GCP,
then you probably want

00:38:26.435 --> 00:38:29.000
to [INAUDIBLE] at the Cloud
Security Command Center, first.

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:31.070
Cloud Security Command
Center is the single pane

00:38:31.070 --> 00:38:34.028
of glass for security
events in your environment.

00:38:34.028 --> 00:38:35.820
For scans that you run
in your environment,

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:37.112
you can the results that apply.

00:38:37.112 --> 00:38:39.365
There are GKE clusters here.

00:38:39.365 --> 00:38:41.240
As you're looking to
further protect yourself

00:38:41.240 --> 00:38:44.075
from potential container
specific attacks,

00:38:44.075 --> 00:38:46.700
Google Cloud already has a range
of container security partners

00:38:46.700 --> 00:38:48.710
integrated with Cloud CSCC.

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:51.180
These look for attacks against
your container workloads

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:54.530
and then alert you
directly, natively in CSCC.

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:57.650
These include Aqua Security,
Capsule8, StackRox,

00:38:57.650 --> 00:38:59.780
Sysdig Secure, and
Twistlock, and we're always

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:03.320
looking to add more to
our partner offering.

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:06.160
So we've talked a lot about a
handful of security features

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:08.660
that you can leverage in Google
Kubernetes Engine and Google

00:39:08.660 --> 00:39:11.130
Cloud, but there's
actually lots more.

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:13.520
GKE also offers features
like private clusters

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:16.328
and pod security policy to help
you protect your workloads.

00:39:16.328 --> 00:39:17.870
Another one that I
didn't talk about,

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:19.287
which I want to
quickly emphasize,

00:39:19.287 --> 00:39:21.470
is binary authorization,
which is inspired

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:23.510
by what Google does
internally to verify code

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:24.585
before it's deployed.

00:39:24.585 --> 00:39:26.210
So you can actually
use that to enforce

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:27.800
what gets put into
your environment

00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:28.940
based on an attestation.

00:39:28.940 --> 00:39:31.640
So for example, to enforce
that, code must come out of your

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:34.160
build pipeline, or it must be
scanned by your vulnerability

00:39:34.160 --> 00:39:35.240
scanner.

00:39:35.240 --> 00:39:37.670
Using these pieces together
leads to a stronger security

00:39:37.670 --> 00:39:39.878
posture, because after all,
strong security is really

00:39:39.878 --> 00:39:42.820
about defense in depth.

00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:45.250
So putting it all
together, let's answer

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:47.750
that initial question
of who protects what.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:50.380
In GKE, Google is responsible
for protecting the control

00:39:50.380 --> 00:39:53.950
plane, which includes
your master VM, etcd,

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:55.120
and controllers.

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:57.670
And you're responsible for
protecting your worker nodes,

00:39:57.670 --> 00:40:00.520
including deploying patches to
the OS, runtime, and Kubernetes

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:05.002
components, and of course,
securing your own workload.

00:40:05.002 --> 00:40:06.460
So what could you
do today to start

00:40:06.460 --> 00:40:08.135
securing Kubernetes on GKE?

00:40:08.135 --> 00:40:10.510
We'd recommend that you stay
at the forefront of security

00:40:10.510 --> 00:40:14.475
by using node auto upgrade,
protecting your workload

00:40:14.475 --> 00:40:15.850
from common image
and application

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:17.410
vulnerabilities
using managed base

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:20.863
images or distroless images,
and GCR Vuln Scanning,

00:40:20.863 --> 00:40:22.780
and following the
"Kubernetes Engine Hardening

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:24.822
Guide" to get on the latest
set of configurations

00:40:24.822 --> 00:40:25.773
that we'd recommend.

00:40:25.773 --> 00:40:27.190
And when you have
a bit more time,

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:29.523
check out some of the additional
content that we covered

00:40:29.523 --> 00:40:31.390
and additional
documents we link to.

00:40:31.390 --> 00:40:33.803
So here is a set of
links to learn more.

00:40:33.803 --> 00:40:35.470
The first one is a
blog post that covers

00:40:35.470 --> 00:40:37.430
the content of today's talk.

00:40:37.430 --> 00:40:39.410
There's a landing page
on container security,

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:41.560
a link to an overview on
security, the hardening guide,

00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:42.510
and the security bulletins.

00:40:42.510 --> 00:40:43.540
That's what I mentioned earlier.

00:40:43.540 --> 00:40:45.620
When there's a new
vulnerability disclosed,

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:47.110
that's where we
post information.

00:40:47.110 --> 00:40:48.040
It has an RSS feed.

00:40:48.040 --> 00:40:50.290
You can use that to post
directly to your team's Slack

00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:52.340
channel, for example.

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:53.927
And then the last
two points there

00:40:53.927 --> 00:40:56.260
are incident response white
paper and our infrastructure

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:59.220
security design white paper.

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:01.080
And if you're at Next
the rest of this week,

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:02.455
check out some of
the other talks

00:41:02.455 --> 00:41:04.065
on container security going on.

00:41:04.065 --> 00:41:05.440
So we're in the
upper left, here.

00:41:05.440 --> 00:41:07.350
But there's a talk
later today on secrets

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:11.460
and on networking, and then
tomorrow on securely accessing

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:13.920
GCP services from Kubernetes.

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:17.280
Thursday, on software supply
chain, policy management,

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:17.910
and gVisor.

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:21.620
That's it for us.

00:41:21.620 --> 00:41:22.953
Thank you so much for your time.

